WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:02.820
- [Narrator] Of the 30
major league Baseball teams,

00:00:02.820 --> 00:00:05.913
26 have infield mixes sourced from here.

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:09.240
- All right, so this is what we're after,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:10.980
the beginnings of a Baseball field.

00:00:10.980 --> 00:00:14.670
All the hard work, crushing
rock, all for this right here.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:17.280
- [Narrator] This right
here is Pittsburgh red bed.

00:00:17.280 --> 00:00:18.840
And this clay stone is unique

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:22.593
in how it reacts to
one key element, water.

00:00:23.490 --> 00:00:24.720
- For the owners of the teams,

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:26.250
it's about playing in the rain.

00:00:26.250 --> 00:00:29.880
You equate it to monetizing
what the cost of the infield was

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:31.950
versus playing a game in the rain.

00:00:31.950 --> 00:00:33.120
- Water's very important

00:00:33.120 --> 00:00:36.630
because it absorbs the ball
bounce, almost like corkboard.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:38.160
- [Narrator] But just getting to the clay

00:00:38.160 --> 00:00:40.020
can take two months.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:43.800
Here's how a single dirt
farm takes all of this,

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:45.453
to turn into this.

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:49.320
It all starts here, 300 million years ago,

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:51.390
sediment from eroding mountains deposited

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.810
this unique rock unit called
the Glenshaw formation

00:00:54.810 --> 00:00:57.570
and the Pittsburgh red
bed is hidden within it.

00:00:57.570 --> 00:01:00.000
- [Grant] We have to remove
all of the overburden above it,

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:02.310
the sandstone, the gray shale

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:05.520
and then a limestone layer
that's about a foot thick

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:06.720
at the bottom.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:08.130
- [Narrator] The
overburden becomes thicker

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:09.720
at higher elevations.

00:01:09.720 --> 00:01:13.020
So DuraEdge products can
only mine for the clay here.

00:01:13.020 --> 00:01:13.980
Otherwise...

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.080
- It's cost prohibitive to mine the clay

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:18.510
because you have too much
overburden to take off the top,

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:20.220
it costs too much money.

00:01:20.220 --> 00:01:22.110
- [Narrator] Pittsburgh red
bed is predominantly made up

00:01:22.110 --> 00:01:24.570
of illite and kaolinite, two clay minerals

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:26.580
that can absorb water without swelling

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.110
and dissipate the water quickly.

00:01:28.110 --> 00:01:31.470
- As you can see, this
material is very densely packed

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.240
so we have to take it up to the plant

00:01:33.240 --> 00:01:35.400
and crush it to make it a usable product

00:01:35.400 --> 00:01:36.900
for Baseball fields.

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:38.040
- [Narrator] Over the course of a year,

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.730
excavators will mine and then
load approximately 30,000 tons

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:43.590
of the clay into these trucks

00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:46.590
driving it all to a processing
facility an hour away.

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:49.350
- The challenge to this
business is taking something

00:01:49.350 --> 00:01:51.270
that's this hard compressed in the ground

00:01:51.270 --> 00:01:53.580
and then ultimately we wanna
get it into that powder.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:55.890
We wanna mill it into
something like talcum powder.

00:01:55.890 --> 00:01:58.320
- The clay is pre
crushed with a bulldozer,

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:00.420
then reduced down to an inch and a half

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.653
thanks to this bucket.

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:04.860
- [Grant] It's got hammers on the inside

00:02:04.860 --> 00:02:08.073
and teeth that grab the clay
and physically smash it.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:11.190
- [Narrator] Finally, the
clay is pushed through

00:02:11.190 --> 00:02:12.510
a REMco crusher.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:15.060
- It's a vertical shaft
impact crusher, and it spins

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:16.650
at 1800 RPMs.

00:02:16.650 --> 00:02:19.080
Between that bucket and that crusher,

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:22.860
it's made the product
perform so much better.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:25.080
- But transforming the
clay into infield mix

00:02:25.080 --> 00:02:29.220
requires a specific proportion
of clay, silt, and sand.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:31.110
- The sand creates void space.

00:02:31.110 --> 00:02:33.060
The silt and clay fill the void space

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:36.240
when it's in the proper ratio,
and that requires the use

00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.440
of a pug mill, which
is back here behind us.

00:02:39.720 --> 00:02:41.880
So what happens when you
put it through the pug mill?

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:44.070
You build friction inside the mixer

00:02:44.070 --> 00:02:46.080
and when you grind the
two products together

00:02:46.080 --> 00:02:48.630
the clay will actually
adhere into the sand

00:02:48.630 --> 00:02:51.480
and it comes out as one
single homogenous mix.

00:02:51.480 --> 00:02:53.100
- [Narrator] The company
creates three types

00:02:53.100 --> 00:02:55.500
of infield mix that vary in proportions.

00:02:55.500 --> 00:02:57.840
Professional grade
infield mix has less sand

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:00.540
and more clay content, which
helps retain moisture better

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.700
but also requires more maintenance.

00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:03.900
- This is a finished product.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:04.920
Once you smooth it off,

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:07.050
you can tell if there's anything in there.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.400
If the sand's too fine,

00:03:08.400 --> 00:03:10.590
I can tell it won't hold
together quite as well.

00:03:10.590 --> 00:03:11.760
If the sand's too coarse,

00:03:11.760 --> 00:03:13.260
the coarse particles will stick out.

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.400
When I'm testing my product

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:15.480
and I've got the right moisture in it

00:03:15.480 --> 00:03:17.880
and I can go like this with my
product, it'll stay together.

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:18.713
You could almost juggle.

00:03:18.713 --> 00:03:20.730
I don't know how to juggle,
but you could juggle

00:03:20.730 --> 00:03:21.750
these balls, right?

00:03:21.750 --> 00:03:24.480
But then when I drop it on the ground

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:26.730
I want that ball to just
completely fall apart.

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:30.930
So then I'll go like this
and I'll pack it down

00:03:30.930 --> 00:03:33.000
and ultimately when it's compressed

00:03:33.000 --> 00:03:35.310
that's what the surface
is gonna look like.

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.440
- [Narrator] Stadiums nearby
will have their infield mix

00:03:37.440 --> 00:03:39.690
delivered from the main
processing facility.

00:03:39.690 --> 00:03:42.630
For stadiums farther away,
only the clay is shipped

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:44.040
and the infield mix is made

00:03:44.040 --> 00:03:46.260
at one of DuraEdge's satellite locations

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:48.840
saving up to 70% in freight costs.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:51.210
- It cuts down tremendously
on logistics costs

00:03:51.210 --> 00:03:52.980
because if you were to
make it all here in ship it

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.190
to Salt Lake City, you would
be shipping five more trucks

00:03:56.190 --> 00:03:58.290
and the cost of that would be too great.

00:03:58.290 --> 00:04:00.540
There are some groundskeepers
that want things

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:03.480
made slightly different, but by and large,

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.900
we're producing the exact
same product for every team.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:08.550
- Like at Citi Field...

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.910
- Citi Field infield mix
is actually very similar

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.380
to a lot of the other ballparks.

00:04:13.380 --> 00:04:15.510
Sometimes regionally they differ slightly

00:04:15.510 --> 00:04:16.710
just based on the weather.

00:04:16.710 --> 00:04:18.930
That's a big factor for us here

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.330
is how our infield mix performs

00:04:21.330 --> 00:04:22.590
in different weather conditions,

00:04:22.590 --> 00:04:25.620
Hot humid, dry, wet rainy.

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.690
You know, we're trying to
get it the same every day.

00:04:27.690 --> 00:04:29.250
- [Narrator] Building a
major league baseball infield

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:33.030
from scratch requires up
to 350 tons of infield mix

00:04:33.030 --> 00:04:37.170
which can cost anywhere
from $50,000 to $80,000.

00:04:37.170 --> 00:04:39.420
So many stadiums focus on maintenance,

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:40.950
incorporating smaller deliveries

00:04:40.950 --> 00:04:43.050
into their existing infield as needed.

00:04:43.050 --> 00:04:45.150
- What's gonna happen
after he's done scarifying

00:04:45.150 --> 00:04:46.980
is we're gonna add a
little bit of material

00:04:46.980 --> 00:04:48.840
and the laser grade's
gonna push the material

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:51.840
into all the little low spots
and cut from the high spots

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:53.370
so that everything's nice and level.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:55.140
He is actually finishing the grade

00:04:55.140 --> 00:04:57.990
of the infield to within
about an eighth of an inch.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.550
- Infield are required to be graded

00:04:59.550 --> 00:05:02.280
so that the baselines and
the home plates are level.

00:05:02.280 --> 00:05:04.260
Then a thin layer of top dressing

00:05:04.260 --> 00:05:07.080
or conditioner is added
to help retain moisture

00:05:07.080 --> 00:05:09.420
and enhance the sliding
surface for players.

00:05:09.420 --> 00:05:12.360
- So this machine right
here is the final step.

00:05:12.360 --> 00:05:14.430
This is a one and a half ton roller

00:05:14.430 --> 00:05:15.960
and this will pack everything back down

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.160
when we're done laser grading.

00:05:17.160 --> 00:05:19.590
After that, a little bit of
water and we're good to go.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.110
- [Narrator] The trick is to
keep the infield consistent

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.820
every single game.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:26.610
- When an infield mix isn't
good, it'll break apart.

00:05:26.610 --> 00:05:28.650
So as players are running around it,

00:05:28.650 --> 00:05:30.900
chunks will be coming out of
it or something's acting up

00:05:30.900 --> 00:05:32.310
then we'll check moisture meter

00:05:32.310 --> 00:05:34.110
and we have a range that we like to get.

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:35.730
It's gonna play well in that range.

00:05:35.730 --> 00:05:37.890
- [Narrator] But roundskeepers
can often check the moisture

00:05:37.890 --> 00:05:39.540
with one simple trick.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:41.460
- If the key goes in and
out nice and smoothly

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:43.350
then we know that it's
where it needs to be.

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:45.780
So the key goes in, it
has a little bit of force

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:47.670
and then it comes out
and there's no chunks.

00:05:47.670 --> 00:05:49.350
It's nice and clean.

00:05:49.350 --> 00:05:51.360
Water's very important for an infield

00:05:51.360 --> 00:05:53.550
because it absorbs the ball bounce.

00:05:53.550 --> 00:05:56.830
It makes the infield mix pliable
and almost like corkboard

00:05:57.780 --> 00:06:00.210
- [Narrator] Before soil science
became standard application

00:06:00.210 --> 00:06:03.690
in the MLB ballparks, teams
relied on local suppliers.

00:06:03.690 --> 00:06:06.690
But groundskeepers say infield
dirt then was inconsistent

00:06:06.690 --> 00:06:08.790
and more difficult to maintain.

00:06:08.790 --> 00:06:11.040
Competing infield mix
suppliers say their products

00:06:11.040 --> 00:06:14.460
achieved the same purpose
without using the specific clay.

00:06:14.460 --> 00:06:17.640
But after starting with the
Philadelphia Phillies in 2005,

00:06:17.640 --> 00:06:20.670
DuraEdge product supplies
most MLB teams today.

00:06:20.670 --> 00:06:23.430
- It started to get out a
bit more around this part

00:06:23.430 --> 00:06:26.280
of the country that, you
know, I'm the dirt guy.

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:28.410
People are incredibly
surprised by the fact

00:06:28.410 --> 00:06:30.150
that this is where it comes from.

00:06:30.150 --> 00:06:32.940
And Presto, whamo, you got infield mix.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:33.963
That's how it works.

